00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:26.630
- [Kelly] I know!

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:28.210
And do you feel like, I know I feel

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:30.470
like it's just like over like
people are just like, yeah.

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:32.320
And I'm like, okay, well what?

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:33.650
- [Mayim] Yeah. It's well and we..

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:34.483
- very confused...

00:00:34.483 --> 00:00:35.930
- and yeah and I think
especially with kid,

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:38.190
I have kids who really
like to follow rules.

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:40.860
So if you give them a
rule, they're gonna take it

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:42.320
all the, like they'll wear a mask

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:43.740
forever if you tell them to.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:45.160
- [Kelly] That's me and my kids,

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:46.210
we're all three the same way.

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:47.680
- [Mayim] So now it's
like, okay, you're fine.

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:49.586
And they're like, mm, but are we?

00:00:49.586 --> 00:00:50.419
(audience laughs)

00:00:50.419 --> 00:00:51.416
- I love it. I love it.

00:00:51.416 --> 00:00:54.530
So, so how, how have
you been since hosting?

00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:55.840
You've been hosting now Jeopardy for

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:56.990
is it over six months now?

00:00:56.990 --> 00:00:59.560
- It, yeah. It's um...

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:02.110
- [Kelly] So, what have
you learned about the game?

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:04.820
- [Mayim] You know, I
I've learned that I still

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:06.300
have a lot to learn.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:07.570
- Oh my God, everyone watching jeopardy.

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:08.403
It's so hard.

00:01:08.403 --> 00:01:10.257
- Well but, but also just like
the, the behind the scenes

00:01:10.257 --> 00:01:12.340
it, to me is so fascinating.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:13.960
Like the people in my ear

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:15.980
and meeting all the
writers and the researchers

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:18.100
and just like getting to
see behind the scenes.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:20.640
If you think you feel dumb
watching the contestants

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.640
wait till you see the
writers and research.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:24.140
Like they're, they're amazing.

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:25.220
They're unbelievable.

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:26.330
And they're in my ear telling me

00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:27.500
all the things I'm doing wrong. All day.

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:29.430
- [Kelly] I have never
been able to do that.

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:30.263
- Very difficult.

00:01:30.263 --> 00:01:32.342
- I am so ADHD. I like literally, if

00:01:32.342 --> 00:01:34.270
if they tried to do that on this show,

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:35.180
I'd be like, what?

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:36.230
- [Mayim] Well I do that a lot.

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:38.970
- Like I, yeah. I would be
like, say again, like what?

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:40.820
And then I'd forget that no one can...

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:42.250
Yep. You feel crazy.

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:44.450
Sometimes I have conversations with Lisa,

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:47.870
the teleprompter goddess here
and the audience has no idea

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:48.790
that she can hear me on my mic so...

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:49.623
- Right?

00:01:49.623 --> 00:01:51.120
- It looks like I'm talking to myself.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:52.657
- Sometimes I do talk to myself, so yeah

00:01:52.657 --> 00:01:54.430
- [Kelly] And I don't care,
I'll look crazy for her.

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:55.550
I love her.

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:58.970
So wait, you recently called
the first round single Jeopardy

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:00.500
instead of just Jeopardy.

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:02.320
So how did people react to that?

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:06.200
- [Mayim] So the, on my, I,
I have basically, you know

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:07.789
clue boards that I review.

00:02:07.789 --> 00:02:08.622
- Yeah.

00:02:08.622 --> 00:02:10.160
- And the first one
does say single Jeopardy

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:11.530
and the second one says double Jeopardy.

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:14.466
- So I didn't make something
up that doesn't exist.

00:02:14.466 --> 00:02:16.940
And people though lost their minds.

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:19.087
So we usually call it the Jeopardy round.

00:02:19.087 --> 00:02:19.950
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:21.460
- And yes, one time we were going

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:22.500
I think to commercial.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:23.333
And I think the thing that

00:02:23.333 --> 00:02:26.070
that I'd like people to remember
is like, I don't go rogue.

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:28.330
It's never like, what is
Mayim Bialik think she...

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:31.690
Like there's 86 people
watching what I'm doing.

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:33.882
Listen, like if they wanted me to redo it

00:02:33.882 --> 00:02:36.900
I would've been happy to redo
it, but no one said anything.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:37.940
I just kept talking.

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:38.773
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:38.773 --> 00:02:39.606
- But I won't do it again.

00:02:39.606 --> 00:02:43.130
'Cause it caused like a whole
like Jeopardy viral situation.

00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:44.480
- [Kelly] Oh my God. Just so you know

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:47.001
I think my team gets
nervous because I go rogue.

00:02:47.001 --> 00:02:48.460
(both laugh)

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:51.160
- I, no with Jeopardy,
I'm very party line.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:52.700
Like, like they're in my ear.

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:55.160
I read the, I do the things, but obviously

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:56.190
that one slipped by.

00:02:56.190 --> 00:02:58.380
- [Kelly] Isn't it funny
what people freak out about?

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:00.010
- [Mayim] People care a lot about Jeopardy

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:01.640
and that's very important.

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:02.960
- [Kelly] I know it is important

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:05.260
but you almost wanna just
like start sending them

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:07.820
like real things that are
happening that are important.

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:10.490
And you're like, you know?
Like don't even respond,

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:13.300
just respond with like, I
don't know, like there's a lot

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:15.080
of homeless people here we
should probably look out for

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:18.750
or I don't know, like there's
like, there's like real issues

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:22.380
and I'm always like, why
did you focus on that?

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:24.507
I don't understand but whatever.

00:03:24.507 --> 00:03:27.230
That was the look at her
new movie: As They Made Us.

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:29.610
So congratulations. You
wrote and directed this.

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:31.290
So is this the first time
you've ever done this?

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:32.123
- Yes I...

00:03:32.123 --> 00:03:33.210
- [Kelly] Were you nervous or excited?

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:34.320
- I was extremely nervous.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:37.070
So I wrote, I've never
written a screenplay.

00:03:37.070 --> 00:03:38.270
I've written other things

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:40.553
but not a dialogue,
screenplay kind of thing.

00:03:40.553 --> 00:03:42.180
I wrote it and...

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:43.490
- [Kelly] I feel like
you're so intelligent,

00:03:43.490 --> 00:03:44.580
you can do anything.

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:47.445
- No, it well, I mean, I appreciate that.

00:03:47.445 --> 00:03:48.770
- [Kelly] It's true.

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:52.250
- No, but like with so many things in life

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:55.268
it's more about like
the time, the balancing,

00:03:55.268 --> 00:03:58.090
the all the other things
you have to do, like

00:03:58.090 --> 00:04:00.550
you know, this was, I assume they

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:03.521
whoever they is would like hire
a writer after I drafted it.

00:04:03.521 --> 00:04:04.430
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:06.220
- No they're like, no, this is the script

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:08.042
and I think we're gonna turn into a movie.

00:04:08.042 --> 00:04:08.875
- [Kelly] That's amazing.

00:04:08.875 --> 00:04:09.790
- Really bananas and I figured,

00:04:09.790 --> 00:04:11.200
okay, well let's find a director.

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:15.660
And then I became the
director and you know, I

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:18.460
I have this, this cast, you know,

00:04:18.460 --> 00:04:20.730
Dustin Hoffman and
Candace Bergen are in it

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:22.566
and Diana Agron and Simon Helberg.

00:04:22.566 --> 00:04:23.986
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:23.986 --> 00:04:25.590
- [Kelly] It's an amazing cast.

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:28.430
- [Mayim] Yeah. That's uh, that was me.

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:30.690
It was really, it was a very surreal...

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:32.710
- Were you nervous directing them

00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:33.980
- Two time Academy award winner?

00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:35.340
Why should I be nervous?

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:36.260
To direct Dustin Hoffman.

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:37.210
- [Kelly] Totally fine.

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:39.970
- No, it was very, it
was very intimidating.

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:42.125
And also there's not a lot of women.

00:04:42.125 --> 00:04:42.958
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:42.958 --> 00:04:44.370
- [Mayim] You know, first time director

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:46.670
and a female and all those things.

00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:48.830
There was, I was very, very intimidated

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:50.060
but actually not pictured there.

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:52.990
Julian Gantt, who is on Call Me Cat.

00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:54.740
I actually cast him in the movie as well.

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:56.670
And we've worked together for, you know

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:58.973
two years now and having him on the cast,

00:04:58.973 --> 00:05:01.290
he plays the caregiver
for Dustin's character

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:03.060
and it was just nice
to have a buddy there.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:03.893
- [Kelly] Absolutely!

00:05:03.893 --> 00:05:05.320
- Like just someone that like knew me

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:06.860
and knew my sense of humor and Simon

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:09.154
obviously I worked with on Big
Bang Theory for nine years.

00:05:09.154 --> 00:05:11.700
So it was also, it was
very comforting, you know

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:14.110
to have people that
know me, know my quirks

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:16.960
and my sense of humor and
my sense of weirdness.

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:19.240
And we filmed in the, the humidist

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:22.320
humidist summer in New Jersey.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:25.440
And mostly what I'm thrilled
about when I watch, you know

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:27.250
the cut of the movie is that we don't look

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:29.820
like we were sweating, which
we were completely covered

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:33.040
in sweat and our hair and
makeup department did amazing

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:35.870
'cause no one looks miserable and sweaty.

00:05:35.870 --> 00:05:38.500
- Wow. Wait, so what, it's a very,

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:40.080
I imagine it was comforting to have people

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:41.080
you know, too, because it's

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:42.820
a very intense subject, the movie.

00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:43.820
Tell everybody what it's about.

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:47.100
- Yeah. So my father of blessed memory

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:48.610
died almost seven years ago.

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:50.080
And in traditional Judaism,

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:52.530
there's a very specific year of grief.

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:55.770
And after that year of grief,
something shifted, which

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:58.280
you know, mystically,
however you wanna look at it.

00:05:58.280 --> 00:06:01.367
And I started seeing these
images like from childhood

00:06:01.367 --> 00:06:05.350
and I was like, I think
I should write them down.

00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:07.010
And that's literally how it started.

00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:09.980
So I grew up in a home with
mental illness, goes back

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:11.660
many generations on both sides.

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:16.010
And I wrote this movie
based on my childhood

00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:18.180
based on a lot of people's childhoods.

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:19.939
There's always like sibling tension

00:06:19.939 --> 00:06:22.555
when there's a parental heaviness.

00:06:22.555 --> 00:06:23.388
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:23.388 --> 00:06:26.590
- And in many families,
there's the sibling who stays

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:27.900
and there's the one that doesn't.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:29.300
- That escapes, almost.

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:31.405
- Correct. And so I wrote a story

00:06:31.405 --> 00:06:33.961
about a family kind of figuring

00:06:33.961 --> 00:06:37.990
out complexity with one
sibling who's very engaged

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:40.720
and the other who has been estranged.

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:41.810
And you know, when people are like,

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:42.762
oh is it your life?

00:06:42.762 --> 00:06:45.660
I've decided that I'm not
gonna go through scene by scene

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:47.850
and be like, this happened
to me, this didn't, you know

00:06:47.850 --> 00:06:49.529
there are things in the
movie that never happened.

00:06:49.529 --> 00:06:50.362
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:50.362 --> 00:06:51.230
- There are things that I've fabricate

00:06:51.230 --> 00:06:53.937
like that's the point of, and
the awkwardness of memory and

00:06:53.937 --> 00:06:56.100
and being a writer, but many people

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:57.520
are raised with mental illness.

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:00.180
Many people are raised in
alcoholism with drug abuse.

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:03.370
So like, to me, these are the
stories that I was trying to

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:05.370
and yeah, it sounds heavy.

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:08.540
It's also funny. Like my
parents were hilarious.

00:07:08.540 --> 00:07:09.373
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:07:09.373 --> 00:07:10.890
- You know, they, there
was trouble in my home.

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:12.600
There was chaos.

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:13.953
It felt unpredictable a lot,

00:07:13.953 --> 00:07:15.147
- [Kelly] It's like dark humor.

00:07:15.147 --> 00:07:18.500
- Right. They were incredibly
loving, hilarious, artistic.

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:21.040
Like I grew up in this amazing home

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:22.840
that was also really challenging.

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:24.392
And I wanted to show in particular

00:07:24.392 --> 00:07:27.050
what happens to siblings
in that situation.

00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:29.820
- [Kelly] I love... was it, I
like 'cause I've written....

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:32.510
It's amazing. I love that
you're talking about it too.

00:07:32.510 --> 00:07:34.710
Cause I think it's something
that's not talked about enough.

00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:35.543
- No, no one talked about it.

00:07:35.543 --> 00:07:36.376
- [Kelly] It's not mainstream enough.

00:07:36.376 --> 00:07:37.980
And I feel like it is an
actual mainstream theme

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:40.370
that happens in a lot of homes
who people don't talk about.

00:07:40.370 --> 00:07:42.530
But for me, like whenever
I write about something

00:07:42.530 --> 00:07:45.010
especially personally like
from my like childhood and I

00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:49.150
and some of it's, you know,
mine that can be scary releasing

00:07:49.150 --> 00:07:50.330
to the world, but also very

00:07:50.330 --> 00:07:52.830
liberating because it's therapeutic.

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:53.663
Is that the same for you?

00:07:53.663 --> 00:07:56.000
- Yeah, I mean, I think for me, I,

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:57.650
I had to gain such distance

00:07:57.650 --> 00:08:00.730
from it because I had to
make things up, you know

00:08:00.730 --> 00:08:02.610
there were things.... So it, it felt

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:04.470
like I had to be more technical about it

00:08:04.470 --> 00:08:06.238
and less emotional if that makes sense.

00:08:06.238 --> 00:08:07.071
- [Kelly] That's kinda cool.

00:08:07.071 --> 00:08:08.260
- And there are things
in the movie, you know,

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:11.459
that are very very gripping and powerful.

00:08:11.459 --> 00:08:14.700
But for me as a director,
like I went straight

00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:17.320
into more of a technical mode, you know

00:08:17.320 --> 00:08:19.510
like I didn't cry while directing.

00:08:19.510 --> 00:08:20.920
And a lot of people on our crew were

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:23.010
very emotional with a
lot of the scenes but...

00:08:23.010 --> 00:08:23.980
- [Kelly] Cause you're still focused

00:08:23.980 --> 00:08:24.813
on doing your job.

00:08:24.813 --> 00:08:26.330
- And I had to be, I
think you have to like

00:08:26.330 --> 00:08:27.520
create that distance...

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:28.353
- Compartmentalization?

00:08:28.353 --> 00:08:29.910
- ...to function. I mean,
like we all do, right?

00:08:29.910 --> 00:08:31.590
- But so you you've taught
a bit of high school,

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:32.690
is that correct? I didn't know that.

00:08:32.690 --> 00:08:35.980
- So yeah, for five years
after I got my doctorate

00:08:35.980 --> 00:08:38.010
before I was made a regular
on the big bang theory

00:08:38.010 --> 00:08:40.071
- [Kelly] You got your
doctorate, who are you?

00:08:40.071 --> 00:08:41.371
I'm just like what? Jesus.

00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:43.880
- No, but for five years

00:08:43.880 --> 00:08:45.390
- You're so impressive. I know you've

00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:46.420
come here like so many times

00:08:46.420 --> 00:08:47.830
and every time I just, I just

00:08:47.830 --> 00:08:48.827
wanna be you every time you're here.

00:08:48.827 --> 00:08:50.640
I'm like, you're so impressive.

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:52.180
- [Mayim] Just lot of
layers to this onion.

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:53.052
